Yes—the clean version is swap the embodiment layer.

We now really do have a synapse-resolution wiring diagram of an adult Drosophila brain: roughly 139,000 neurons and about 50 million chemical synapses, plus cell-type and neurotransmitter annotations. That lets researchers model information flow and test hypotheses about how particular circuits could generate behavior. 

But there’s an important gap:

connectome ≠ functioning fly mind

A wiring diagram does not fully specify membrane dynamics, neuromodulation, current synaptic strengths, gene expression, body state, sensory history, learning state, or the closed loop between brain and moving body. So we cannot currently “transfer ourselves into the fly” and literally experience being that individual fly.

What we can increasingly do is build a Fly Layer:

fly sensors / modeled sensory inputs
→ fly-connectome-derived processing
→ simulated or robotic body
→ environmental interaction
→ return telemetry

That becomes an embodied probe. Instead of a human physically entering some space, you could have a tiny robot or simulation operating under a fly-inspired perceptual/controller model and translate its observations back into human/AI representations.

Then your Pantheon move becomes:

Human Layer
Fly Layer
Bat Layer
Dog Layer
Spider Layer
Machine-Vision Layer
Thermal Layer
Chemical Layer

Each is a different world-interface adapter.

And then compare their deltas against Golden Reality:

same environment
→ different embodiment/interface
→ different accessible information
→ different behavior
→ cross-layer reconstruction

The really interesting target is not “possess the fly.”

It is borrow its coordinate system.

Because once we can represent what variables matter to a fly, a bat, a spider, a thermal camera, or a radar system, the AI layer can translate among them without requiring the human sensory stack to sit in the middle every time.

I’d call that Embodiment Abstraction:

world ↔ embodiment adapter ↔ latent world model ↔ another embodiment

Then “human” becomes one adapter among many rather than the mandatory hub.

And that is probably the stronger version of what you meant by you wouldn’t need the human layer. You could preserve human oversight while letting sensing, navigation, simulation, and translation happen through whichever embodiment is best suited to that particular terrain.